  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Graphite & Brass",
    "overview": "Worthwatch is a private instrument, not a dashboard. The palette is warm charcoal, with bone-coloured text and a single brass accent. Brass is spent on one thing per screen: the net worth line, the current state, or the one action that matters. Every figure is set in a monospaced face with tabular numerals, so columns of money line up like a ledger. Dark is the home theme. Light is a warm paper version of the same system, not a separate design.\n\nThe interface is dense and quiet. Screens are a single centred column, 820px wide (1360px on Changes), made of bordered surfaces on a slightly darker ground. Information is encoded visually wherever it can be: sparklines on every account row, stacked group bars, a waterfall bridge, a treemap, a fan chart, coloured status dots. Words are reserved for labels and the occasional sentence of explanation.\n\nThe system is coherent in its colours and figures. It is generic in its scaffolding: the bordered card, the uppercase micro-label and the segmented pill appear on every screen. This document records the system as it ships today, before any redesign.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Warm graphite ground with bone text. Dark-first, with an equal light theme.",
      "One brass accent, kept rare.",
      "Fixed group colours: cash blue, investments orange, property green, debt violet. They mean the same thing on every chart.",
      "Money set in IBM Plex Mono with tabular numerals. Prose in Instrument Sans.",
      "Flat, bordered surfaces. Depth comes from tone, not shadow.",
      "Small type: most UI text is 11–13px."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Brass Rule",
        "body": "Brass marks the single most important line or state on a screen. Bars and cells without a category use the neutral `--fill`, never brass.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Fixed Meaning Rule",
        "body": "A group colour means its group on every screen. Never reuse cash blue or debt violet for anything else.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ledger Rule",
        "body": "Any number a person might compare is set in the figures face with tabular numerals.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Ground Rule",
        "body": "Resting surfaces never cast shadows. Only layers that float above the page, such as the palette and tooltips, get one.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do set every amount in `var(--figures)` with tabular numerals.",
      "Do take colours only from `tokens.css`. Components contain no hex literals.",
      "Do keep brass for the one primary line, state or action on a screen. Use `--fill` for neutral bars.",
      "Do use the fixed group colours for cash, investments, property and debt on every chart.",
      "Do mark unconfirmed data with the 135° hatch, not a new colour.",
      "Do design both themes. Every token has a light value.",
      "Do respect `prefers-reduced-motion`."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't add shadows to resting cards or rows.",
      "Don't use a sector colour outside the treemap, or a group colour for anything but its group.",
      "Don't introduce a second accent hue.",
      "Don't write \"worthwatch\" in lowercase in visible UI. The brand is \"Worthwatch\"."
    ]
  }
}